## About JPA

JPA (Java Persistence API) is a specification that defines how Java objects can be mapped to relational databases. It provides standard annotations and interfaces but does not contain any implementation.

Example:

```java
@Entity
public class Employee {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Integer id;

}
```

---

## About Hibernate

Hibernate is an ORM framework that implements the JPA specification. It converts Java objects into database records and automatically generates SQL queries.

Without Spring Data JPA, developers need to write code to manage sessions and transactions manually.

Example:

```java
Session session = factory.openSession();

Transaction transaction = session.beginTransaction();

session.save(employee);

transaction.commit();

session.close();
```

---

## About Spring Data JPA

Spring Data JPA is built on top of JPA. It simplifies database operations by providing repository interfaces.

Instead of writing session management code, we only need a single method call.

Example:

```java
employeeRepository.save(employee);
```

---

## Comparison

| JPA | Hibernate | Spring Data JPA |
|-----|-----------|-----------------|
| Specification | ORM Framework | Spring Module |
| No implementation | Implements JPA | Uses JPA internally |
| Defines standards | Performs ORM operations | Reduces boilerplate code |
| Requires an implementation | Manages persistence | Provides Repository interfaces |

